sants  so we need to be able to come up with a  way to  quickly assess the immune status of  these patients and treat them  appropriately  a second major question is a lot of  these hospitalized covenanting patients  they are making neutralizing antibody  and yet they're really sick  and so we need to understand why that  neutralizing antibody  is not able to control the virus in  these patients  the third question is what i just  brought up which is why are these  patients  really so sick is it immune pathology is  it um  viral mediated um we still don't  understand  and it's probably very different for  each individual patient  and sort of the last interesting thing  that i think about a lot is if are these  differences  related to kinetics there's some  emerging evidence that suggests  that early viral control is very  important  and part of this is perhaps an  interferon response  so it's possible that the patients  that end up getting very sick and end up  requiring hospitalization  are patients that when infected with  cyrus kobe 2  fail to control the virus  spread early and and then it basically  turns into a runaway train  but understanding the kinetics of this  response in different donors is going to  be really important  so in the last few minutes i'm going to  tell you a little bit about our new work  which is trying to understand the immune  responses to covid and kids  so at the same time that we are  collecting samples for our adult cohort  we are also collecting samples from two  different pediatric cohorts  at the children's hospital of  philadelphia  we had one cohort which is the cobit 19  cohort  and this cohort was divided up into two  groups  one that experienced ards acute  respiratory distress syndrome  and the second that did not so this  helped  basically as a measure of severity for  copenhagen disease in this pediatric  group  the second group were a group of  pediatric patients that were diagnosed  with ms c  so miss c is stands for multi-system  inflammatory syndrome in children  the cdc definition here are individuals  that are  less than 21 years of age they have to  present with fever  inflammation and multi-system organ  involvement and also have a saris cov2  positive test but the experience  at chop has been that these are kids  that are  you know even robustly healthy  absolutely no  previous sickness and then they get  really really sick  and some of the most common features  here are  fever rash gi involvement  and a significant portion of these kids  complain  of a very acute abdominal pain  and occasionally some of these patients  and we do know have had family members  they have document documented infection  three to four weeks prior to this misty  diagnosis  so this is sort of what the caseload  looked like in philadelphia  the adult covid cases peaked around late  mid to late april and then basically  three to four weeks later we had this  increase in misc diagnosis um  cases at the children's hospital  and so just this timing alone sort of  raises some interesting questions  um first of all were these missy  patients basically infected with  steroids kobe 2 around the same time as  the adults  but then have had some kind of long  chronic infection  and if that's true what what  then is the cause for them to develop  these acute symptoms  is there a secondary event such as  another viral infection  um reactivation of a of you know sort of  an adonis chronic viral infection like  cmv  or some other kind of secondary event  maybe autoimmune  so just a note about the analysis for  this cohort  our primary statistical comparisons here  are misc  versus the pediatric covin-19 but what  was really great was because all of  these samples were  collected stained and processed at the  same time as the adult  we could then actually use the adult  sample  as basically a comparator and this  helped us have a framework for  understanding  these immune responses both in the  pediatric coveted as well as the missy  so just like the adults we had various  features of clinical metadata  so what i'm showing you on the left hand  side are  two different measures of serum  inflammation so ferritin  and d-dimer and you can see that the  misc kids here in the green triangles  have all have elevated levels of both of  these serum proteins indicating global  inflammation  and on the right hand side you're  looking at the all lymphocyte count  and most of the missing kids are below  the the healthy donaire range and are  clinically lymphopenic  we can now use our high dimensional flow  cytometry to study the immune response  and first we're looking at these major  immune subsets  and what we see in the missy kids is a  little bit different from what we saw  in both the adult copen19 and the  pediatric  covid19 which is an increase in the  proportion of b  cells but then similar to the adults  the misc are decreased in the proportion  of t  cells with the cd4 t cells but more  dramatically the cda t  cells and we can see that the misty  kids here basically look like the  sickest adults in terms of the percent  of cdht cells  so now we're going to look at the t cell  activation again using some of those  same markers we described earlier what  we can see  is that um there there is a small  percent of  activated cd4 and cd8 t cells in the  pediatric  19 patients but if you look at the misc  examples here there's a huge expansion  of both the activated cd4  and cda t cells in circulation and this  is enumerated on the right hand side  and so if you look down on the bottom  specifically at the cdat cells  the miss c patients have either on par  or even greater  levels of cd8t cell activation compared  to the sickest adults  so one of the things that we are  interested in was to try to understand  if these cd8 t-cells were not just  activated but perhaps  actually exhibiting signs of t-cell  exhaustion  and this was we thought an intriguing  question especially because of that  timing i talked about earlier between  the  when we saw the covet 19 adult cases  come in much earlier in april and then  this  missy sort of wave coming in three to  four weeks later indicating that  these kids may have been infected for  quite a while and so t-cell exhaustion  is a state that can happen in chronic  viral infection in cancer  when t-cells are chronically stimulated  and then eventually become dysfunctional  so the first way that we assess this was  to look at the inhibitory receptor  pd-1 and so we saw that indeed  our misc patients had an increase in  pd-1 expression  both in the cd4 t cells shown here on  the top and the cd8 cells shown on the  bottom  compared to the pediatric covid19  and we know that pd-1 by itself is not a  good  indication of exhaustion because it can  also be transiently  increased in activated t-cells so we  looked at a secondary  inhibitory receptor cd39  and while it didn't reach statistical  significance our misc patients  the majority of them did have increased  city 39 compared to the pediatric cohort  and really one of the characteristic  signs of t-cell exhaustion  is the high expression of multiple  inhibitory receptors  and we know specifically that  co-expression of pd-1 and cd39  marx exhausted t cells an adult chronic  infection like hiv  as well as cancer so then we examined  the co-expression of both of these  inhibitory receptors in the citigate t  cells and that's what we see here  and indeed there is a statistically  there's a statistical increase in the  number of cd t  cells expressing both of these  inhibitory receptors in the misty  patients  so the last thing we wanted to  understand here uh was again how the  immune system was changing over time  and this was uh particularly interesting  in these missy patients because when  these kids first show up the hospital  they're really sick and in a scary kind  of way  but luckily with the right treatments  these  individual patients almost always  recover  and are able to be discharged and do  just fine and so we wanted to understand  what the immune system looks like at  those different time points when they  came in and they were really sick  and also uh what they looked like when  their clinical symptoms were improving  so we had three different misty patients  where we were able to collect  two different time points and ask how  the immune system was changing  over that time  so since the cd8t cell activation  response was sort of one of the most  remarkable things  about these patients we first looked at  the  the percent of cd 38 and dr positive  cells  at the first draw compared to the second  draw so that's what you see here  and each row represents one donor so at  the first draw you see this very  elevated  acidity t-cell response that is  significantly decreased by the second  draw  and that's enumerated here on the on the  top plot  and then we looked at another sorry  several other  t cell activation markers including k-67  which denotes these proliferating cells  we looked at pd-1  and also looked at the change in these  double positive pd-1  city 39 double positive cells and  basically all of these various measures  of t cell activation decrease between  those two  time points we then asked how the  clinical  features were changing over those time  points  and so if you look at the top left hand  side here that's the olympic site count  so as i noted at the beginning the miss  c kids tend to have  lower lymphocyte counts and some are  lymphopenic but here what we can see by  that second time point that lymphocyte  count has actually recovered to almost  healthy normal domain range  we can also look at these various  measures of inflammation  and sort of the take-home message here  is that while  many of the kids do not necessarily  reach the healthy donor range  you are starting to see a decrease in  this global  this global sort of inflammation that we  see  and i'll just summarize um this last  part of the talk uh and what i've shown  you here  is that the syndrome misty is  characterized by very high t-cell  activation  and this really rivals uh what we see in  the sickest covid19  adult i've also shown you some evidence  that the cd8t cells msc may be exhausted  but it's going to take a lot more work  to determine if that's true  and i've also shown you that while  there's a resolution of clinical  symptoms  this does coincide with the decrease in  t cell activation  and of course that sort of leaves a lot  of questions about what the pathology of  this disease is  and whether this is just sort of  coincidental  or if this disease is actually mediated  in part by this high degree of t cell  activation  and i'll just point out of course so  much more work to be done here  with this with this misty syndrome  and with that um i would like to  acknowledge all of my many collaborators  of course john wary my p.i um  and underlined here uh in the weary lab  i've listed all of the the  co-6 authors on the original science  paper  and in green here are my my co-authors  that have  contributed to the work on the miss c  this whole covet blood processing unit  on the bottom  and i want to point out amy baxter  really built this from the ground up  in the middle of the pandemic it was a  huge effort  and lots of people contributed really  volunteered their time  newla meyer who initiated all this work  in the clinical side  many many other labs at penn including  at the children's hospital of  philadelphia  and i'll just thank my funding sources  especially of course cri  and then mark foundation and i'm happy  to take any questions  thank you josephine for your informative  presentation  we would now like to start the live q a  portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  does hydroxychloroquine alter the immune  response in coca-19 adult patients  um yep that's an interesting question so  some of our patients i think about  45 did receive that treatment  there were some nominal associations  with some of the various forms of  t-cell activation but that probably is  more to do with  just how sick those patients were and  not necessarily any direct effect of the  drug itself  okay great thank you the next question  is  what types of pre-existing conditions do  the adult patients have  i'm actually really glad you asked xa i  forgot to mention it  um so most of our most of our adult  cohort had significant comorbidities  um i believe about 80 of them had some  kind of  cardiovascular pre-existing condition  i know about 40 also had diabetes um 45  were obese and so our adult  covid19 uh cohort had a lot of these  underlying comorbidities  and this is really interesting because  it was sort of in stark contrast to what  we saw with  the miss c patients where those kids had  absolutely no underlying conditions and  were really robustly healthy  so that was sort of an interesting  contrast in the two sort of severe forms  of the disease that we're seeing  okay thank you and our next question is  do serum antibody levels correlate with  disease severity  um surprisingly they do not at all  okay and do the miss see patients  have cobia covered antibodies  right um so i didn't show that data  today um  but yes they do so we've measured uh  again um  antibody directed towards the spike  protein uh in the cyroscopy ii  uh virus and all of our  missy kids are seropositive and it's  actually a defining feature of the  disease  oh wow okay we've got one more question  does the cd4 t cell response also change  over time in the misc patients  right um so the misc patients were  characterized by both high  cd8 and cd4 responses um  and yes the the answer is yes we also  see a decrease over time from that  that first blood draw to the second  blood draw in  the the cda and the cd4 responses  all right well thank you again josephine  for your time today and for your  important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor beckmann coulter life  sciences for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye


